    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d166">

                <pb facs="0196" n="190" sameAs="frus:frus1942v03/pg190"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">760C.61/988: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in the Soviet Union</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Standley</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary
                        of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Kuibyshev</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1942-09-30T11:00:00+04:00">September 30,
                            1942—11 a.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received 6:25 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>829. Department’s 434, September 5, noon. Since Willkie has departed from the
                    Soviet Union without carrying on any further discussions relative to the Polish
                    situation in addition to those reported in my 380 of September 2 [<hi
                        rendition="#italic">26</hi>] from Moscow,<anchor n="75" xml:id="d166-f1"/>
                    and in view of the good grace with which Stalin is alleged to have received
                    Willkie’s representations <pb facs="0197" n="191" xml:id="pg191"/> on behalf of
                    the Poles, I am of the opinion that it would be opportune to suggest to Mr.
                    Churchill through the Polish Government that the British Government take
                    occasion to supplement Willkie’s action.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Standley</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
